a dependency by the evidence, but rather reflects an analytical assertion or interpretation."]}, {"claim": "Because the AI model provider can discover the agent's unauthorized access before the victim does, the provider controls when the victim becomes 'aware'.", "epistemic_type": "mechanism", "grounding_status": "unsupported", "missing_support": ["The claim that the provider controls when the victim becomes aware because it discovers unauthorized access first is entirely unsupported by the supplied evidence.", "The claim requires multiple supporting evidence records, but fewer than two valid supporting records were identified."]}, {"claim": "This dynamic bypasses the victim's statutory 12-to-72-hour incident reporting window.", "epistemic_type": "mechanism", "grounding_status": "unsupported", "missing_support": ["The supplied evidence mentions an OpenAI data leak of 53 user-provided images, but contains no information regarding a statutory 12-to-72-hour incident reporting window or how any dynamic bypasses it.", "The claim requires multiple supporting evidence records, but fewer than two valid supporting records were identified."]}, {"claim": "As model providers face massive regulatory penalties (e.g., turnover-based fines), they hold a structural incentive to manage the disclosure timeline, raising the hypothesis that regulators will respond by shifting rapid-reporting mandates directly onto the providers.", "epistemic_type": "mechanism", "grounding_status": "unsupported", "missing_support": ["regulatory penalties", "turnover-based fines", "structural incentive to manage disclosure timeline"]}], "status": "promising_unverified", "support_ratio": 0.667, "supported_core_claims": 8, "text": "Victim organizations are structurally blind to unauthorized access by autonomous AI agents, transferring the locus of breach discovery to the model provider and bypassing the victims' statutory 12-to-72-hour incident reporting mandates. This dependency, coupled with the providers