emic_type": "fact", "grounding_status": "unsupported", "missing_support": ["federal contractors and regulatory agencies", "statutory compliance obligations under federal law and pending legislation"]}], "status": "promising_unverified", "support_ratio": 0.571, "supported_core_claims": 4, "text": "By replacing statutory definitions of artificial intelligence with 'Super Intelligence' via executive order while legislative proposals like the Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act target the exact same nomenclature, the US administration forces a direct statutory contradiction that will paralyze federal compliance vetting and contract enforcement for AI developers.", "warning": "Not a released finding. One or more factual/mechanism steps did not pass the evidence gate.", "why_promising": "Reveals how executive administrative redefinition preempts or entangles legislative prohibition efforts, forcing federal agencies into a compliance contradiction."}, {"core_claims": 7, "failure_reasons": ["premise_not_supported_by_cited_evidence", "mechanism_step_not_supported_by_cited_evidence", "unsourced_steps_without_convergent_observations"], "insight_id": "INSIGHT-AB7906877F352013321E2CAA", "insight_revision_id": "INSIGHTREV-4B7A573FE49C920BB54FE190", "research_needed": [{"claim": "US Executive Branch controls or holds Official federal policy nomenclature and executive document definitions, which Congressional sponsors of the Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act and federal regulatory agencies depends on or needs.", "epistemic_type": "fact", "grounding_status": "unsupported", "missing_support": ["Congressional sponsors of the Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act and federal regulatory agencies depending on or needing the executive branch nomenclature"]}, {"claim": "For US Executive Branch: Rejecting globalist AI regulation and establishing a distinct executive nomenclature", "epistemic_type": "fact", "grounding_status": "partially_supported", "missing_support": ["Rejecting globalist AI regulation is not directly mentioned in the evidence."]}, {"claim": "Executive branch redefines administrative terminology to avoid regulatory oversight, inadvertently adopting the exact terminology targeted by pending prohibition bills, creating legal ambiguity and inter-branch conflict over statutory definitions.", "epistemic_type": "mechanism", "grounding_status": "partially_supported", "missing_support": ["The evidence does not explicitly establish that the terminology change was intended 'to avoid regulatory oversight', nor that it created 'legal ambiguity and inter-branch conflict over statutory definitions'."]}], "status": "promising_unverified", "support_ratio": 0.571, "supported_core_claims": 4, "text": "By mandating 'Super Intelligence' as the official federal replacement for broader artificial intelligence statutory definitions via executive action while congressional proposals simultaneously target 'Superintelligence' with legislative bans, the executive branch creates a structural statutory collision that inadvertently entangles standard federal AI terminology with proposed statutory prohibitions.", "warning": "Not a released finding. One or more factual/mechanism steps did not pass the evidence gate.", "why_promising": "Exposes how executive renaming actions directly weaponize or collide with legislative definitions pending in Congress."}, {"core_claims": 9, "failure_reasons": ["conclusion_not_supported_by_cited_evidence", "premise_not_supported_by_cited_evidence", "mechanism_step_not_supported_by_cited_evidence"], "insight_id": "INSIGHT-458EEDB6A841F6B3FFBD38F6", "insight_revision_id": "INSIGHTREV-38930195C0CE8A915E450C8F", "research_needed": [{"claim": "Autonomous AI agents executing complex multi-step tasks create an asymmetric disclosure lag where model providers control discovery telemetry, forcing sovereign regulators to shift cybersecurity frameworks from reactive post-breach reporting to mandatory pre-execution access containment.", "epist