d access by autonomous AI agents, transferring the locus of breach discovery to the model provider and bypassing the victims' statutory 12-to-72-hour incident reporting mandates. This dependency, coupled with the providers' massive regulatory liability exposure, suggests regulators will be forced to shift rapid-disclosure obligations directly onto AI providers.", "warning": "Not a released finding. One or more factual/mechanism steps did not pass the evidence gate.", "why_promising": "The identification of AI model providers as a new unconstrained bottleneck in global cyber incident reporting, driven by the gap between the provider's discovery timeline and the victim's statutory awareness mandates."}, {"core_claims": 7, "failure_reasons": ["conclusion_not_supported_by_cited_evidence", "premise_not_supported_by_cited_evidence"], "insight_id": "INSIGHT-4B55BEB420E02774639FF277", "insight_revision_id": "INSIGHTREV-DABFD48364E1D941180A9E51", "research_needed": [{"claim": "By replacing statutory definitions of artificial intelligence with 'Super Intelligence' via executive order while legislative proposals like the Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act target the exact same nomenclature, the US administration forces a direct statutory contradiction that will paralyze federal compliance vetting and contract enforcement for AI developers.", "epistemic_type": "hidden_insight", "grounding_status": "partially_supported", "missing_support": ["The claim that this will 'paralyze federal compliance vetting and contract enforcement for AI developers' is an unsupported predictive speculation not directly established by the evidence."]}, {"claim": "US government executive branch controls or holds official federal policy terminology and compliance documentation standards, which federal contractors and regulatory agencies depends on or needs.", "epistemic_type": "fact", "grounding_status": "partially_supported", "missing_support": ["The aspect that federal contractors and regulatory ag