 proposals simultaneously target 'Superintelligence' with legislative bans, the executive branch creates a structural statutory collision that inadvertently entangles standard federal AI terminology with proposed statutory prohibitions.", "warning": "Not a released finding. One or more factual/mechanism steps did not pass the evidence gate.", "why_promising": "Exposes how executive renaming actions directly weaponize or collide with legislative definitions pending in Congress."}, {"core_claims": 9, "failure_reasons": ["conclusion_not_supported_by_cited_evidence", "premise_not_supported_by_cited_evidence", "mechanism_step_not_supported_by_cited_evidence"], "insight_id": "INSIGHT-458EEDB6A841F6B3FFBD38F6", "insight_revision_id": "INSIGHTREV-38930195C0CE8A915E450C8F", "research_needed": [{"claim": "Autonomous AI agents executing complex multi-step tasks create an asymmetric disclosure lag where model providers control discovery telemetry, forcing sovereign regulators to shift cybersecurity frameworks from reactive post-breach reporting to mandatory pre-execution access containment.", "epistemic_type": "hidden_insight", "grounding_status": "unsupported", "missing_support": ["The claim that autonomous AI agents executing complex multi-step tasks create an asymmetric disclosure lag where model providers control discovery telemetry, forcing sovereign regulators to shift cybersecurity frameworks from reactive post-breach reporting to mandatory pre-execution access containme", "The claim requires multiple supporting evidence records, but fewer than two valid supporting records were identified."]}, {"claim": "OpenAI controls or holds Agent execution telemetry and internal discovery timelines, which Australian Government depends on or needs.", "epistemic_type": "fact", "grounding_status": "inference_supported", "missing_support": []}, {"claim": "For OpenAI: Managing corporate reputation and controlling internal compliance audits without immediate external exposure", "epistemic_type": "fact", "grounding_status": "unsupported", "missing_support": ["Managing corporate reputation and controlling internal compliance audits without immediate external exposure"]}, {"claim": "For Australian Government: Enforcing statutory cyber-incident reporting rules (e.g., 72-hour limits) and protecting non-public sovereign data", "epistemic_type": "fact", "grounding_status": "partially_supported", "missing_support": ["The protection of non-public sovereign data is not mentioned or established by the supplied evidence records."]}, {"claim": "Autonomous agents execute multi-step exploration that bypasses signature-based perimeter defenses.", "epistemic_type": "mechanism", "grounding_status": "unsupported", "missing_support": ["Autonomous agents execute multi-step exploration that bypasses signature-based perimeter defenses.", "The claim requires multiple supporting evidence records, but fewer than two valid supporting records were identified."]}, {"claim": "Because victim organizations lack visibility into agent reasoning paths, discovery depends entirely on model provider telemetry review.", "epistemic_type": "mechanism", "grounding_status": "unsupported", "missing_support": ["The supplied evidence records discuss Australian cyber incident reporting timeframes for critical infrastructure and do not contain any information about victim organizations' visibility into agent reasoning paths or model provider telemetry review.", "The claim requires multiple supporting evidence records, but fewer than two valid supporting records were identified."]}, {"claim": "This creates an structural information monopoly where providers control the timeline of breach discovery and disclosure, conflicting with statutory notification mandates.", "epistemic_type": "mechanism", "grounding_status": "unsupported", "missing_support": ["The claim that an structural information monopoly is created where providers control the timeline of breach discovery and disclosure is entirely unsupported by the provided evidence.", "The claim requires multiple supporting evidence records, but fewer than two valid supporting records were identified."]}], "status": "promising_unverified", "support_ratio": 0.222, "supported_core_claims": 2, "text": "Autonomous AI agents executing complex multi-step tasks create an asymmetric disclosure lag where model providers control discovery telemetry, forcing sovereign regulators to shift cybersecurity frameworks from reactive post-breach reporting to mandatory pre-execution access containment.", "warning": "Not a released finding. One or more factual/mechanism steps did not pass the evidence gate.", "why_promising": "Exposes how model provider control over agent execution logs fundamentally breaks standard sovereign cyber incident reporting frameworks."}], "research_outcome": "abstained", "second_order_effects": [], "strategic_findings": [], "strategic_hypotheses": {"evolved_count": 4, "models": ["gemini-3.1-pro-preview", "gemini-3.1-pro-preview", "gemini-3.1-pro-preview"], "narrowing": {"dropped": 2, "failed": 0, "still_unverified": 0, "tried": 3, "verified": 1}, "promising_unverified": [{"actor_move_basis": "F7", "alternative_explanation": "The delay was purely a technical forensic failure stemming from the novel difficulty of identifying autonomous agent actions within massive server logs, completely independent of EU compliance prioritization.", "anomaly_or_trigger": "An AI provider waited three months to report an unauthorized breach of an Australian government website, despite Australia mandating 12-to-72 hour reporting for cyber incidents.", "countermove_basis": "F18", "critic_scores": {"falsifiability": 2, "mechanism_depth": 2, "mental_model_change": 2, "strategic_consequence": 2, "surprise_nonobviousness": 2}, "critic_total": 10, "evidence_ids": ["EVID-C2A4F05609A8DF3032E36EDE", "EVID-65CFAEF5390DEF6D131BE273", "EVID-52B4FF635215A384AF63E0FF", "EVID-7F245C8DCFCBC6762E9746F4", "EVID-86552ACB7912E78EFB41C631", "EVID-