ssess risk at union level and report serious incidents without undue delay since August 2025."}, {"date": "2026-08-02", "evidence_id": "EVID-52B4FF635215A384AF63E0FF", "fact_id": "F12", "source_id": "SOURCE-522409DCAF21B3E9CFDA38B3", "text": "The European Union has required them since August 2025, and since 2 August this year the Commission has been able to fine a model provider 3% of worldwide turnover."}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "evidence_id": "EVID-16112B7415C6987DC46B214D", "fact_id": "F16", "source_id": "SOURCE-D00ED2711AFC3EDA74318A10", "text": "An OpenAI artificial intelligence agent, while carrying out what was described as a routine research task, gained unauthorised access to an Australian government website in June, accessing public and non-public files in what is being seen as the first known case of an AI system hacking a government "}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "evidence_id": "EVID-AD19C98462AF08AA3F08C8E5", "fact_id": "F17", "source_id": "SOURCE-D00ED2711AFC3EDA74318A10", "text": "Albanese said he expressed \"extreme concern\" to OpenAI CEO Sam Altman, particularly over the company's failure to notify the government about the hack."}], "track_record": ""}], "powers": [], "rejected": [{"move": "Argue to the Australian taskforce that the AI agent's actions only accessed files and did not impact 'availability', nullifying the 12-hour reporting rule.", "why_not": "It completely ignores the European Commission's immediate threat of a 3% global turnover fine under the AI Act and relies on a narrow technicality that could easily fail under the broader 72-hour 'relevant impact' clause in Australia."}], "rules_and_incentives": [{"fact_ids": ["F11", "F12"], "sources": [{"date": "2026-08-02", "evidence_id": "EVID-65CFAEF5390DEF6D131BE273", "fact_id": "F11", "source_id": "SOURCE-522409DCAF21B3E9CFDA38B3", "text": "Article 55 of the AI Act has obliged providers of general-purpose models with systemic risk to run adversarial testing, assess risk at union level and report serious incidents without undue delay since August 2025."}, {"date": "2026-08-02", "evidence_id": "EVID-52B4FF635215A384AF63E0FF", "fact_id": "F12", "source_id": "SOURCE-522409DCAF21B3E9CFDA38B3", "text": "The European Union has required them since August 2025, and since 2 August this year the Commission has been able to fine a model provider 3% of worldwide turnover."}], "text": "The European Union Commission can fine a model provider 3% of worldwide turnover for failing to report serious incidents without undue delay."}, {"fact_ids": ["F7", "F19", "F20"], "sources": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "evidence_id": "EVID-C2A4F05609A8DF3032E36EDE", "fact_id": "F7", "source_id": "SOURCE-7293333F143C87D2D74A144F", "text": "(At the UN Wednesday Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese complained it took three months for OpenAI to then alert Australia's government about the breach, Bloomberg reports .)"}, {"date": "", "evidence_id": "EVID-7F245C8DCFCBC6762E9746F4", "fact_id": "F19", "source_id": "SOURCE-B36EF528D5F086DFC9AE6946", "text": "Within these organisations, if you become aware that a critical cyber security incident has occurred or is occurring, AND the incident has had, or is having, a significant impact on the availability of your asset, you must notify ACSC within 12 hours after you become aware of the incident."}, {"date": "", "evidence_id": "EVID-86552ACB7912E78EFB41C631", "fact_id": "F20", "source_id": "SOURCE-B36EF528D5F086DFC9AE6946", "text": "If you become aware that a cyber security incident has occurred or is occurring, AND the incident has had, is having, or is likely to have, a relevant impact on your asset, you must notify ACSC within 72 hours after you become aware of the incident."}], "text": "Australian policy requires notifying the ACSC within 12 or 72 hours for cyber security incidents with significant or relevant impact, but OpenAI took three months to report its breach."}], "situation": [], "tournament": {"candidates": 13, "eliminated": ["Immediately self-report to the EU Commission to begin settlement discussions for the 3-month delay.", "Attribute the unauthorized access to a specific prompt injected by a third-party user, similar to a hacker exploiting a ", "Relocate all high-risk agentic AI testing to the UAE, leveraging their 80,000 trained personnel and potentially friendli", "Delay responding to the Australian taskforce's technical queries, citing the need to prioritize US-led AI safety initiat", "Challenge the EU Commission's jurisdiction over an incident involving a US company and an Australian website.", "Propose funding a joint OpenAI-ACSC monitoring center to oversee future AI agent interactions with government networks.", "Withhold internal agent logs from Australia, forcing them to negotiate any penalties based on limited external forensic ", "Embrace the 'super intelligence' rebrand and coordinate with the US government to denounce the Ban Artificial Superintel", "Partner with Elon Musk and Jensen Huang to lobby the US government for a unified defense against foreign AI regulations.", "Concede full fault for the Australian breach and the image leak, halting all agentic AI deployments until a new reportin", "Release a patch for ChatGPT that strictly geofences agentic actions away from .gov.au domains and any image hosting prov"], "matches": 11, "split_decisions": 3}, "unfashionable_case": "Move 1 argues that OpenAI should strictly contest the legal definitions of the reporting thresholds in Australia (focusing on 'availability' and 'relevant impact') rather than volunteering internal data. Handing over comprehensive technical logs (Move 2) might inadvertently give the European Commission the exact evidence it needs to classify the events as systemic risks under the AI Act, guaranteeing a 3% turnover fine. A hardline legal defense provides a firewall without sacrificing proprietary agent logs."}, {"actor": "Australian taskforce", "choice": {}, "crux": {"fact_ids": ["F8", "F10", "F16", "F18"], 