 sponsors of the Ban Artificial Superintelligence Act and federal regulatory agencies depending on or needing the executive branch nomenclature"]}, {"claim": "For US Executive Branch: Rejecting globalist AI regulation and establishing a distinct executive nomenclature", "epistemic_type": "fact", "grounding_status": "partially_supported", "missing_support": ["Rejecting globalist AI regulation is not directly mentioned in the evidence."]}, {"claim": "Executive branch redefines administrative terminology to avoid regulatory oversight, inadvertently adopting the exact terminology targeted by pending prohibition bills, creating legal ambiguity and inter-branch conflict over statutory definitions.", "epistemic_type": "mechanism", "grounding_status": "partially_supported", "missing_support": ["The evidence does not explicitly establish that the terminology change was intended 'to avoid regulatory oversight', nor that it created 'legal ambiguity and inter-branch conflict over statutory definitions'."]}], "status": "promising_unverified", "support_ratio": 0.571, "supported_core_claims": 4, "text": "By mandating 'Super Intelligence' as the official federal replacement for broader artificial intelligence statutory definitions via executive action while congressional proposals simultaneously target 'Superintelligence' with legislative bans, the executive branch creates a structural statutory collision that inadvertently entangles standard federal AI terminology with proposed statutory prohibitions.", "warning": "Not a released finding. One or more factual/mechanism steps did not pass the evidence gate.", "why_promising": "Exposes how executive renaming actions directly weaponize or collide with legislative definitions pending in Congress."}, {"core_claims": 9, "failure_reasons": ["conclusion_not_supported_by_cited_evidence", "premise_not_supported_by_cited_evidence", "mechanism_step_not_supported_by_cited_evidence"], "insight_id": "INSIGHT-458EEDB6A841F6B3FFBD38F6", "insight_re