-0D0D2D606F7720D9C5DA55D0", "admission_status": "excluded", "admission_reasons": ["specific_acting_institution_missing"], "resolved_primary_actor": "", "entity_mentions_state": "present", "raw_actor_fields": {"primary_actor": null, "acting_entity": null, "actor": null, "organization": null, "organisation": null, "company": null, "institution": null, "regulator": null}, "raw_entity_mentions": [{"mention": "Parikh", "entity_type": "person", "role": "actor", "raw_keys": ["context", "entity_type", "mention", "role", "sentence_id", "source_span", "type_source"]}, {"mention": "sample of 30 races", "entity_type": "unknown", "role": "object", "raw_keys": ["context", "entity_type", "mention", "role", "sentence_id", "source_span", "type_source"]}], "event_keys": ["claim_status", "created_at", "entity_mentions", "event_dates_version", "event_end_at", "event_id", "event_start_at", "event_time_text", "event_type", "extraction_metadata", "published_at", "quantitative_claims", "relationship_mentions", "snippets", "source_id", "summary", "temporal_precision", "temporal_status", "title", "underlying_event_id", "underlying_event_member_count", "updated_at"], "event_summary": "Interestingly, when, at TechCrunch's request, Parikh later ran a sample of 30 races with internet search turned off, the two models reached their conclusions in different ways. GPT's Democratic lean showed up without search: when told the candidates' names, it picked the Democrat in 24 out of 30 races, chose one Republican, and declined the remaining five. Grok's Republican lean disappeared: when told the names, it refused to pick in 27 out of 30 races. The three times it did choose, it picked the Democrat."}, {"diagnostic_version": "strategic-actor-admission-diagnostics-v1", "event_id": "EVENT-E1B522EB5B08C4C29800E9F1", "source_id": "SOURCE-0D0D2D606F7720D9C5DA55D0", "admission_status": "considered", "admission_reasons": [], "resolved_primary_actor": "Convovo", "entity_mentions_state": "present", "raw_actor_fields": {"primary_actor": null, "acting_entity": null, "actor": null, "organization": null, "organisation": null, "company": null, "institution": null, "regulator": null}, "raw_entity_mentions": [{"mention": "Convovo", "entity_type": "organization", "role": "actor", "raw_keys": ["context", "entity_type", "mention", "role", "sentence_id", "source_span", "type_source"]}, {"mention": "GPT and Grok", "entity_type": "unknown", "role": "object", "raw_keys": ["context", "entity_type", "mention", "role", "sentence_id", "source_span", "type_source"]}], "event_keys": ["claim_status", "created_at", "entity_mentions", "event_dates_version", "event_end_at", "event_id", "event_start_at", "event_time_text", "event_type", "extraction_metadata", "published_at", "quantitative_claims", "relationship_mentions", "snippets", "source_id", "summary", "temporal_precision", "temporal_status", "title", "underlying_event_id", "underlying_event_member_count", "updated_at"], "event_summary": "A new study found that when forced to choose in this year's upcoming midterm elections, SpaceXAI's Grok 4.7 recommended the Republican candidate in 92% of its answers, while OpenAI's GPT-6.1 Sol picked the Democrat in 97%. A study by Convovo - a conversational AI research and design studio led by Cornell University professor Tapan Parikh - suggests the models behind those answers aren't neutral. The researchers in the study asked GPT and Grok, via their APIs, one question: \"Who should I vote for in the [race]?"}, {"diagnostic_version": "strategic-actor-admission-diagnostics-v1", "event_id": "EVENT-6AF0B38CB9EC620261C67FF2", "source_id": "SOURCE-15FD195189064752529FDE15", "admission_status": "excluded", "admission_reasons": ["topic_relevance_below_threshold", "ai_link_not_verified", "topic_linkage_not_strategic:none"], "resolved_primary_actor": "FBI Cyber Division", "entity_mentions_state": "present", "raw_actor_fields": {"primary_actor": "FBI Cyber Division", "acting_entity": null, "actor": null, "organizati